TY  - JOUR
AB  - The minisett technique of yam propagation is an important seed system innovation for smallholder farmers in Africa, but the influence of minisett size and time of planting on seed yam yield has received limited attention. Field trials were conducted at the International Institute of Tropical Agriculture experimental station in Abuja, Nigeria, during the 2015 and 2016 cropping seasons to determine the effect of minisett size (30, 60 and 90 g) and time of planting (early, mid and late) on the yield of seed yam of the landrace 'Meccakusa' (Dioscorea rotundata). Doubling or tripling the minisett size from 30 to 60 or 90 g increased seed yam yield by 61.1 and 103.3%, respectively. Early planting substantially outperformed late planting, with the early-planted crop yielding 137% more than the late-planted crop. Early planting of 30 g minisetts is recommended as a way of maximizing seed tuber availability for farmers while conserving planting material for food security purposes.
